# Cache policy for the static assets.
#
# ── Why this file exists ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Five separate times, a change was deployed, verified live by fetching it,
# and still not visible in a browser. Every time the answer was the same:
# CF-Cache-Status: HIT on the document, serving an older copy of the HTML
# while the same URL with a query string returned the current one.
#
# The pages already sent `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which asks a
# cache to check before reusing. Something at the edge was reusing anyway.
# Rather than keep purging by hand, the HTML says outright that it is not to
# be stored.
#
# ── The trade ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# HTML is the small part and the part that changes. Every request now reaches
# the origin for it, which costs a few milliseconds and is worth more than the
# alternative — which is shipping a change and having no way to know whether
# anyone can see it.
#
# Everything with a stable name goes the other way: images and icons are
# immutable for a year, because a screenshot that changes gets a new file name
# rather than new bytes under the old one.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# ── The documents ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three headers saying one thing, because the edge was reading the first and
# doing as it pleased: every response for /app/ came back
# `Cache-Control: no-store` AND `CF-Cache-Status: HIT`, which is Cloudflare
# storing a document it was told not to store.
#
# They are not duplicates. Cache-Control is the instruction to the browser,
# CDN-Cache-Control is the instruction to any intermediate cache, and
# Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control is Cloudflare's own and outranks both at its
# edge. Sending only the first left the one cache that was misbehaving as the
# one cache that had never been addressed directly.
#
# Why it matters more than a stale page: edge caches are per-location. A
# deploy can be current in one city and a week old in another, so "I can see
# it" and "I cannot" are both true at once and neither tells you anything.
# That is not a cache problem, it is a problem with knowing whether a change
# shipped.
#
# If HIT survives this, the cause is upstream of the origin's headers — a
# Cache Rule with Edge TTL set to ignore cache-control, which overrides all
# three and can only be changed in the dashboard.
/
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
  Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
  Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
/app/
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
  Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
/app/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
  Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
/privacy/
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
  Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
/terms/
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate
  CDN-Cache-Control: no-store
  Cloudflare-CDN-Cache-Control: no-store

# Named assets. These are replaced by adding a file, never by editing one.
/brand/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/shots/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
